// Flagship articles, extra depth (part 2): World War II, the Roman Empire, Ancient Egypt, Manila, Filipino food and rice.
window.DEEP3=window.DEEP3||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP3,{
ww2:{s:[
["Blitzkrieg","On 1 September 1939, Germany invaded Poland, and Britain and France declared war two days later. Using fast-moving tanks and dive bombers in a style called blitzkrieg, or lightning war, Germany conquered Denmark, Norway, the Netherlands, Belgium and France in 1940. British troops were rescued from the beaches of Dunkirk by a fleet of naval and civilian boats. By mid-1940, Britain stood almost alone against Germany in western Europe."],
["Pearl Harbor","On 7 December 1941, Japanese aircraft attacked the US Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor in Hawaii, sinking or damaging many warships and killing about 2,400 Americans. Within hours Japan also attacked the Philippines, Malaya, Hong Kong and other places. The United States declared war on Japan, and Germany and Italy declared war on the United States, turning the conflict into a true world war."],
["Turning points","In 1942 and early 1943 the tide turned against the Axis. In June 1942 the US Navy sank four Japanese aircraft carriers at the Battle of Midway. At El Alamein in Egypt, British and Commonwealth forces defeated Germany’s Afrika Korps. At Stalingrad, the Soviet Red Army surrounded and destroyed a German army in a brutal winter battle that ended in February 1943."],
["Victory in Europe","On 6 June 1944, D-Day, Allied troops landed on the beaches of Normandy in France and began liberating western Europe, while Soviet forces pushed west. In April 1945, Soviet troops reached Berlin, and Adolf Hitler killed himself. Germany surrendered unconditionally, and 8 May 1945 was celebrated as Victory in Europe Day."],
["The atomic bombs","On 6 August 1945, the United States dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima, killing tens of thousands of people instantly, and on 9 August another on Nagasaki. The Soviet Union declared war on Japan on 8 August. Japan announced its surrender on 15 August, and the formal surrender was signed aboard the USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945, ending the war. The bombings remain the only use of nuclear weapons in war."],
["The cost of war","World War II was the deadliest conflict in history. Between about 70 and 85 million people died, a majority of them civilians, including about 6 million Jews murdered in the Holocaust. The Soviet Union and China suffered the greatest losses. The war left cities in ruins, created millions of refugees and led to the founding of the United Nations."]]},
rome:{s:[
["Legends of Rome’s founding","According to legend, Rome was founded in 753 BCE by Romulus, who, with his twin Remus, had been raised by a she-wolf. Archaeology shows that villages stood on Rome’s hills by about the 800s BCE and that the city grew under Etruscan kings. In 509 BCE, the Romans drove out their last king and founded a republic, governed by elected officials and the Senate."],
["Conquering the Mediterranean","Rome slowly conquered Italy, then fought three wars against Carthage, a powerful city in North Africa. In the Second Punic War, the Carthaginian general Hannibal crossed the Alps with elephants and won great victories, but Rome survived and finally destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE. By the end of the republic, Rome ruled lands all around the Mediterranean, which Romans called Mare Nostrum, our sea."],
["Roads and aqueducts","Romans built about 80,000 kilometers of paved roads to move armies, goods and messages across the empire, giving rise to the saying all roads lead to Rome. Aqueducts carried fresh water from distant hills into cities for fountains, baths and toilets. Roman concrete, made with volcanic ash, was so strong that buildings such as the Pantheon, with its huge dome, still stand after nearly 1,900 years."],
["Bread and circuses","Rome’s population may have reached about a million people. The emperors kept them content with free grain and spectacular entertainment, which the poet Juvenal mocked as bread and circuses. The Colosseum, opened in 80 CE, held about 50,000 spectators for gladiator fights and animal hunts. Chariot races at the Circus Maximus drew even bigger crowds."],
["The empire at its height","Under the emperor Trajan, around 117 CE, the empire reached its greatest size, stretching from Britain to Mesopotamia and home to perhaps 60 million people. Hadrian, who followed him, built a great wall across northern Britain. The two centuries from Augustus to Marcus Aurelius are called the Pax Romana, the Roman peace, a time of relative stability and prosperity."],
["The Eastern Empire","In 330 CE the emperor Constantine founded a new capital, Constantinople, on the site of ancient Byzantium. After the empire split, the eastern half, known today as the Byzantine Empire, lasted for another thousand years. Its emperor Justinian collected Roman law in a great code that still influences legal systems today. Constantinople fell to the Ottoman Turks in 1453, ending the Roman Empire."]]},
egypt:{s:[
["Unification","According to tradition, around 3100 BCE a king named Narmer, or Menes, united Upper Egypt in the south and Lower Egypt in the north, founding the first dynasty. The Narmer Palette, a carved stone slab, shows him wearing the crowns of both lands. Egyptian kings later wore a double crown combining the white crown of the south and the red crown of the north."],
["The Old Kingdom","The Old Kingdom, from about 2686 to 2181 BCE, is known as the Age of the Pyramids. The first, the Step Pyramid at Saqqara, was designed by the architect Imhotep for King Djoser. The Great Pyramid at Giza, built for Khufu around 2560 BCE, was made of about 2.3 million stone blocks. The Great Sphinx, with a lion’s body and a king’s face, guards the Giza pyramids."],
["The New Kingdom","During the New Kingdom, from about 1550 to 1070 BCE, Egypt became an empire, conquering lands in Nubia and the Levant. Famous rulers included Hatshepsut, a woman who ruled as pharaoh; Akhenaten, who worshipped one sun god, the Aten; the boy king Tutankhamun; and Ramesses II, who built huge temples such as Abu Simbel and reigned for 66 years."],
["Farming and the flood","Each summer, the Nile flooded, spreading rich black silt across the fields. Egyptians called their land Kemet, the black land, after this soil, and the desert Deshret, the red land. Farmers grew wheat, barley, flax and vegetables, and used canals and the shaduf, a bucket on a pole, to water their crops. The yearly flood was so important that the calendar was based on it."],
["Science and medicine","The Egyptians developed a 365-day calendar, mathematics for measuring fields and building pyramids, and some of the earliest known medical writings. The Edwin Smith Papyrus describes how to treat wounds and broken bones. Egyptian doctors set bones, stitched cuts and used honey, which kills germs, on wounds."],
["Rediscovering Egypt","After ancient Egyptian writing fell out of use, its meaning was forgotten for centuries. In 1799 French soldiers found the Rosetta Stone, which carried the same decree in hieroglyphs, demotic script and Greek. Using it, Jean-François Champollion deciphered hieroglyphs in 1822, opening the history of ancient Egypt to the modern world. The discovery of Tutankhamun’s tomb in 1922 sparked worldwide fascination."]]},
manila:{s:[
["Maynila before 1571","Before the Spanish came, Maynila was a fortified Muslim settlement at the mouth of the Pasig River, ruled by Rajah Sulayman and linked by trade and family to the Sultanate of Brunei. Across the river stood Tondo, ruled by Lakandula. Both traded with Chinese, Japanese and Southeast Asian merchants. The name Maynila may come from nilad, a mangrove plant that grew along the shore."],
["The city of churches","Spanish Manila was filled with churches and convents. San Agustin Church in Intramuros, finished in 1607, is the oldest stone church in the country and part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site of the Baroque Churches of the Philippines. Manila Cathedral has been rebuilt several times after fires, earthquakes and war. Quiapo Church is home to the Black Nazarene."],
["Escolta and the old downtown","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, Escolta Street in Binondo was Manila’s most fashionable shopping street, with department stores, banks, cinemas and the first elevators in the country. Elegant Art Deco and Beaux-Arts buildings lined its sidewalks. After World War II, business moved to Makati, but artists and heritage groups are now reviving Escolta."],
["The American plan","After 1898 the American colonial government hired the architect Daniel Burnham to plan a modern Manila. His 1905 plan proposed wide boulevards, parks and government buildings around Luneta, now Rizal Park. Buildings such as the Manila Post Office, the National Museum buildings and the Manila Hotel date from this period. Roxas Boulevard, along Manila Bay, became famous for its sunsets."],
["A crowded city","Manila is one of the most densely populated cities in the world, with more than 40,000 people per square kilometer. Its streets are filled with jeepneys, tricycles and buses, and traffic is a daily challenge. Light rail lines, the LRT, opened in 1984, and a subway for Metro Manila is under construction. Many residents live in informal settlements, and providing housing is a major challenge."],
["Culture and learning","Manila is home to many of the country’s oldest universities, including the University of Santo Tomas, founded in 1611, and the University of the Philippines, founded in Manila in 1908 before moving mostly to Diliman. The National Museum of Fine Arts displays Juan Luna’s Spoliarium. The Cultural Center of the Philippines, on reclaimed land along Manila Bay, hosts theater, dance and music."]]},
phfood:{s:[
["Sinigang and sour flavors","Filipinos love sour flavors. Sinigang, a soup soured with tamarind, guava, kamias or green mango, is cooked with pork, shrimp or fish and vegetables such as kangkong and radish. Paksiw cooks fish or meat in vinegar. Kinilaw, raw fish cured in vinegar or calamansi with ginger, onion and chili, is one of the oldest Filipino dishes; archaeologists found evidence of it in Butuan more than 1,000 years old."],
["Vinegar, soy and fish sauce","Filipino cooking relies on a few key condiments. Vinegar is made from coconut sap, sugarcane or nipa palm, each with its own taste. Soy sauce came with Chinese traders. Patis, fish sauce, and bagoong, fermented fish or shrimp paste, add savory umami. At the table, people mix their own dipping sauces, called sawsawan, from vinegar, soy sauce, calamansi, chili and garlic."],
["Chinese influence","Chinese traders and settlers brought noodles, spring rolls and steamed buns. Pancit, from a Hokkien word, became a birthday favorite because long noodles stand for long life. Lumpia, siopao and siomai are everyday foods. Many dishes have Hokkien names, such as pancit, lumpia and the cooking style tokwa’t baboy, showing how deeply Chinese cooking shaped Filipino food."],
["Spanish and Mexican influence","Through more than 300 years of Spanish rule and the Manila galleons, Filipinos adopted dishes such as paella, which became bringhe, lechon, menudo, caldereta, leche flan and ensaimada. Ingredients from the Americas, including tomatoes, corn, chili, cacao, peanuts and potatoes, arrived by galleon from Mexico. Many of these dishes became fiesta foods, cooked for special occasions."],
["American influence","The American period brought sandwiches, fried chicken, hot dogs, canned goods and ice cream. Spaghetti became Filipino spaghetti, sweet and topped with sliced hot dogs and cheese. Jollibee, founded in 1978, turned these tastes into a fast-food empire that now has stores in many countries and competes with McDonald’s in the Philippines."],
["Rice at every meal","For most Filipinos, a meal is not complete without rice. Garlic fried rice, sinangag, is eaten at breakfast with longganisa sausage, tapa or tocino, forming dishes called silog, from sinangag and itlog, egg. Rice is also ground into flour for kakanin, sweet rice cakes such as bibingka, puto and suman, and is eaten with every kind of ulam, the main dish."]]},
rice:{s:[
["Wet and dry rice","Most rice is grown in flooded fields called paddies, where water helps control weeds and pests. This is called lowland or wet rice farming. Upland rice is grown on dry hillsides, relying on rain, often in shifting cultivation. In some places farmers grow deepwater rice, which can stretch its stems to keep its leaves above floods several meters deep."],
["The Green Revolution","In the 1960s, scientists at the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños, Laguna, developed IR8, a short, sturdy rice variety that produced far more grain when given fertilizer and water. Called miracle rice, it helped double or triple harvests across Asia and prevented famines. This rise in food production is known as the Green Revolution."],
["A thirsty crop","Growing rice uses a great deal of water, roughly a third of the world’s irrigation water. To save water, scientists developed a method called alternate wetting and drying, in which fields are allowed to dry out for a few days between floodings. Flooded paddies also release methane, a greenhouse gas, so this method helps the climate too."],
["Rice and the economy","Rice prices are an important political issue in many Asian and African countries. Countries such as the Philippines, China, Indonesia, Nigeria and Saudi Arabia import large amounts, while India, Thailand, Vietnam and Pakistan are the biggest exporters. Governments use stockpiles, tariffs and subsidies to keep rice affordable while supporting farmers."],
["Climate-ready rice","Typhoons, floods, droughts and saltwater from rising seas threaten rice fields. Scientists have bred varieties that can survive being underwater for two weeks, such as those carrying the Sub1 gene, as well as rice that tolerates drought and salt. These varieties help farmers in Bangladesh, India and Southeast Asia keep harvesting despite extreme weather."],
["Rice in culture","Rice is woven into culture across Asia. In Japan, rice cakes called mochi are eaten at New Year, and Bali’s rice terraces are watered by subak, a temple-based system recognized by UNESCO. Harvest festivals celebrate rice from India’s Pongal to the terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras, and in many places children are taught never to waste a single grain."]]}
});

;
